<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Proof By Cases</title>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Big Idea</title>

            <p>
                <q> Proof by Cases </q> is a type of direct proof.
                We use proof by cases when we might not have enough hypotheses
                (like the whole hypothesis is <q><m>n</m> is an integer</q> ),
                or if the conclusion suggests cases (like <q>...is even</q> or
                <q>...is a type 1 integer </q> ).
                Proof by Cases works when you break down the problem and do
                <q> sub proofs</q> that will <q>cover</q> the whole hypothesis.
                E.g.., if the hypothesis is <q> <m>n</m> is an integer</q> then
                you could do 2 sub proofs - one where <m>n</m> is an even
                integer and one where <m>n</m> is an odd integer.
                Since all integers are either even or odd, you've proven the
                result for all integers!
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <proposition>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For all integers <m>n</m>,
                    <md>
                        1+(-1)^n(2n-1)
                    </md>
                    is even.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>

        <p workspace="3in">
            <em>Pre-proof analysis:</em>
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Definition Uses Cases? Try Proof by Cases!</title>

            <p>
                What is <m>|7|</m>?
                What is <m>|-4|</m>?
            </p>

            <definition>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For <m>x\in\mathbb{R}</m>, we define <m>|x|</m>, called
                        the <term>absolute value of <m>x</m></term>, by
                        <md>
                            |x| = \begin{cases} x \amp \text{ if } x\geq 0\\ -x \amp \text{ if } x&lt;0 \end{cases}.
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <activity workspace="1in">
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Check that this definition agrees with your answers
                            for <m>|7|</m> and <m>|-4|</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Does the following conjecture seem true?
                            Test it out!
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            <term> Conjecture </term> For all real numbers
                            <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, <m>|xy| = |x||y|</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <p>
                        What cases might make sense to use here?
                        What should you prove in each case?
                        If time - pick one case and try it!
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>